EXACT PUBLIC SURFACE:

internal static class WrittenTypeScanner
{
    internal static void Register(
        CompilationStartAnalysisContext context,
        Action&lt;SyntaxNodeAnalysisContext, INamedTypeSymbol&gt; inspect)
    {
        context.RegisterSyntaxNodeAction(
            nodeContext =&gt; Dispatch(nodeContext, inspect),
            SyntaxKind.IdentifierName,
            SyntaxKind.GenericName);
    }

    internal static void RegisterForAssembly(
        CompilationStartAnalysisContext context,
        string assemblyName,
        Action&lt;SyntaxNodeAnalysisContext, INamedTypeSymbol&gt; inspect)
    {
        if (!string.Equals(context.Compilation.AssemblyName, assemblyName, StringComparison.Ordinal))
        {
            return;
        }
        Register(context, inspect);
    }

    private static void Dispatch(
        SyntaxNodeAnalysisContext context,
        Action&lt;SyntaxNodeAnalysisContext, INamedTypeSymbol&gt; inspect)
    {
        if (context.SemanticModel.GetSymbolInfo(context.Node, context.CancellationToken).Symbol is INamedTypeSymbol type)
        {
            inspect(context, type);
        }
    }
}

This is MemberUseScanner's own Register/RegisterForAssembly shape (analyzers/AgentGuard.Analyzers/MemberUseScanner.cs:43-73), generalized from "resolve a member use" to "resolve a written type name" — same gate-then-scan wiring so a caller gated to one compilation spells the gate once regardless of which lens it scans with, same delegate-supplies-predicate-and-diagnostic contract. Two registrations total, no new using beyond System/Microsoft.CodeAnalysis/Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp/Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.Diagnostics.

SYNTAX KINDS: SyntaxKind.IdentifierName and SyntaxKind.GenericName. Nothing else — not QualifiedName, not TypeOfExpression, not NameOf (the three other candidates the ground explorer's grep probed for, per ground-facts.json's analyzer-fence claim at line 21-22).

WHY those two suffice for every position the contract lists (typeof, nameof, cast, is/case pattern, generic type argument, generic constraint, base/interface list, attribute argument, using alias, array/pointer element, every declaration position): in the Roslyn syntax tree, every one of those positions bottoms out at a leaf SimpleNameSyntax (IdentifierNameSyntax or GenericNameSyntax) node, and RegisterSyntaxNodeAction visits EVERY node of a registered kind in the tree regardless of nesting depth — not just top-level occurrences. A cast to List&lt;CrossPlatformAdapters&gt; contains "CrossPlatformAdapters" as its own independently-visited IdentifierNameSyntax nested inside the outer GenericNameSyntax's TypeArgumentList; typeof(CrossPlatformAdapters[])'s element type is its own IdentifierNameSyntax nested in an ArrayTypeSyntax; nameof(AgentGuard.Engine.SystemServices)'s argument parses as a MemberAccessExpressionSyntax chain whose rightmost .Name is its own IdentifierNameSyntax; a using alias's target QualifiedNameSyntax has its rightmost .Right as its own IdentifierNameSyntax; a generic constraint's TypeConstraintSyntax.Type is a TypeSyntax that is itself Identifier/GenericName when non-generic. I traced all eleven contract-listed positions against SystemServices.Create()'s live body (src/AgentGuard.Boundaries/SystemServices.cs:86-107) and confirmed this holds for the two concrete cases that exist there today: "CrossPlatformAdapters" at line 90 is BOTH the local's declared-type node AND the invocation receiver — two separate IdentifierNameSyntax leaves, both independently visited, both needing the identical (isDoorType && insideCreate) test; "PlatformServices" at line 91 appears only as the invocation receiver inside a global::-qualified chain and is still caught as the chain's rightmost leaf.

WHY NOT TypeTree.Any: the hint framing this angle names TypeTree.Any for "the generic/array/pointer walk," but TypeTree.Any (analyzers/AgentGuard.Analyzers/TypeTree.cs:25-45) recurses over an already-resolved ITypeSymbol's structure — it exists because DeclaredTypeScanner/AG0006 starts from ONE resolved symbol (a field's .Type, a local's inferred type) with no corresponding per-nested-position syntax node to visit separately, so the only way to reach a type buried in its generic arguments is to walk the symbol tree by hand. The written-name lens starts from SYNTAX, not a pre-resolved symbol, and syntax already gives a distinct node per nested position (proven above) — GetSymbolInfo on the outer GenericNameSyntax "List&lt;CrossPlatformAdapters&gt;" resolves to List&lt;CrossPlatformAdapters&gt; itself (whose Name is "List", not a match), while the separately-visited inner "CrossPlatformAdapters" node resolves directly. Adding a TypeTree.Any call on top of leaf-node registration would not catch anything the leaf visit doesn't already catch on its own — it would be inert, redundant machinery bolted on because the hint suggested it, not because the coverage requires it. I verified there is no existing analyzer in this project that registers this way today (ground-facts.json analyzer-fence claim, line 21: grep for SyntaxKind.IdentifierName|GenericName|QualifiedName|TypeOfExpression|NameOf across the whole project returns zero hits), so this is genuinely new machinery either way; the leaf-registration form is the smaller amount of it. I did keep the other two reuse instructions in the hint: the Check(context, TypeSyntax)-then-GetSymbolInfo shape from ContractConcreteTypeMustNotBeCastToAnalyzer.cs:82-89 is exactly what Dispatch generalizes (resolve-then-hand-to-caller, just parameterized instead of hardcoded to one Rule/one predicate), and WellKnownType.IsInAssembly (WellKnownType.cs:66-71) is what each CONSUMING rule's own predicate calls to recognize its one exempted door