CONTRACT BOOKKEEPING THIS PROPOSAL IMPLIES: contract.md's Surfaces list already names DeclaredTypeScanner.cs as a new file (line list under "analyzers/AgentGuard.Analyzers/EngineAssembly.cs, ... DeclaredTypeScanner.cs, EngineToBoundariesOneDoorAnalyzer.cs, and EngineInternalsOneDoorAnalyzer.cs — new files") but cannot yet name mine, since naming it was this stage's open question. Once accepted, CONTRACT must add WrittenTypeScanner.cs to that same line — a mechanical amendment, not a new decision, since the contract already approved that a second file would exist and only reserved its name.

PROPOSAL: one new file, `analyzers/AgentGuard.Analyzers/WrittenNameScanner.cs`, `internal static class WrittenNameScanner` in `namespace AgentGuard.Analyzers` — sibling to `MemberUseScanner.cs`, `TypeTree.cs`, and the not-yet-built `DeclaredTypeScanner.cs`. It is the written-name half of the split Tim already approved; `DeclaredTypeScanner` stays the carried-type half, unchanged by this proposal.

THE MECHANISM. C# has exactly four syntax kinds that hold a name written in source — `IdentifierName`, `GenericName`, `QualifiedName`, `AliasQualifiedName` — and every type-or-member reference the language allows, in every position, bottoms out in one of them. I did not take this on faith: I wrote a throwaway console probe (`Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp` 4.14.0, the same major version this repo already restores), parsed real C# covering all eleven contract-named positions plus a pointer element, and printed each matching node's kind, parent, and `GetSymbolInfo` resolution. Verified findings: `typeof(X)`'s argument, `nameof(X)`'s argument (both the bare and the dotted form), a cast's target, an `as` target, an `is`/`case` pattern's type, a generic type argument, a generic constraint (`where T : X`), a base/interface-list entry, an array element, a pointer element, and every declaration position (field/property/method-return/parameter/local, including inferred `var`) all resolve correctly through these four kinds with zero special-casing per position. An attribute argument needs no separate handling at all: C# only allows a `System.Type` attribute argument spelled as `typeof(X)`, so it is the same `typeof` path, not a fifth one. `nameof`'s dotted form (`nameof(AgentGuard.Engine.SystemServices)`) parses as a nested `MemberAccessExpressionSyntax` chain of plain `IdentifierName`s (expression grammar), not `QualifiedName` (type grammar) — confirmed by the probe, and worth stating because it is easy to assume the wrong node kind here. A doc-comment `<see cref="X"/>` is deliberately NOT covered: `RegisterSyntaxNodeAction` does not descend into structured trivia by default, and the contract's own required-position list never names a cref.

Because resolution goes through the semantic model (`SemanticModel.GetSymbolInfo`), not node kind or text, this is complete BY CONSTRUCTION: a fifth consumer needing a sixth position (say, a `RefTypeSyntax`/`TupleTypeSyntax` element some future rule cares about) needs NO change to this file — those positions already resolve through the same four kinds today. This is the exact opposite of AG0007's existing shape (`ContractConcreteTypeMustNotBeCastToAnalyzer.cs:49-52`), which registers one `RegisterSyntaxNodeAction` per position (cast/as/two patterns) and would need a new registration for every new position — precisely the "enumerated kind list the next position silently escapes" pattern GROUND found AG0007 already exhibits for its own type-set, and which this design does not repeat for Engine-internals/CrossPlatform-core/per-OS types.

THE CORRECTNESS DETAIL (also verified, not assumed). A dotted or alias-qualified name is one logical reference spread across nested nodes that ALL resolve to the identical symbol — in `A.B.C`, the outer `QualifiedName` and its trailing `C` identifier both resolve to `C`. Visiting every one blindly would multiply-report a single reference (confirmed: my probe caught this for real on `AgentGuard.Engine.SystemServices` used as a field type — the outer `QualifiedName` and the nested `SystemServices` identifier both independently resolved to the same `NamedType`). The fix is a parent-based skip test — skip a node that is the `.Left`/`.Right` of an enclosing `QualifiedNameSyntax`, or the `.Name` of an enclosing `AliasQualifiedNameSyntax` — which I proved collapses to exactly one report per reference even at depth: I compiled `AgentGuard.Engine.SomeGeneric<AgentGuard.Engine.SystemServices>` as a field type and confirmed the skip rule correctly walks past the outer qualified name, the wrapping qualified generic, and their namespace segments, landing on exactly the one inner qualified type-argument node that resolves to `SystemServices` — nothing skipped that shouldn't be, nothing missed that should be caught. This skip logic is not optional polish; omitting it silently inflates every diagnostic count the three consumers' acceptance tests assert against a qualified reference.

THE PUBLIC SURFACE, mirroring `MemberUseScanner`'s existing two-tier shape exactly so a rule already calling `MemberUseScanner.RegisterForAssembly` for its member-access half calls this the same way for its type-reference half:

```csharp
// Copyright (c) 4thWAIV. All rights reserved.

using System.Collections.Immutable;
using Microsoft.CodeAnalysis;
using Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp;
using Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp.Syntax;
using Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.Diagnostics;

namespace AgentGuard.Analyzers;

/// <summary>
/// Resolves every name the source WRITES — an identifier, a generic name, a dotted qualified name, or an
/// alias-qualified name — to the symbol it binds to, and hands each one to a rule's inspector already resolved
/// (the written-name lens). C# routes every type-or-member name through exactly one of these four syntax kinds,
/// regardless of position: a declaration, a <c>typeof</c>, a <c>nameof</c> argument, a cast, an <c>as</c>, an
/// <c>is</c>/<c>case</c> pattern, a generic type argument, a generic constraint, a base/interface list entry, an
/// attribute argument (a <c>System.Type</c> argument can only be spelled as <c>typeof(...)</c>, so this is the same
/// path, not a fifth one), a using-alias target, and an array or pointer element all bottom out here. ONE
/// registration therefore reaches every position the language allows; a rule needing a NEW position covered needs
/// no change here and no registration of its own. Resolution is semantic (Roslyn's own name binding), never a node
/// kind or text match, so an alias, a fully-qualified spelling, and a bare identifier all resolve the same way.
/// This is the written-name counterpart to <see cref="DeclaredTypeScanner"/> (reads a symbol's ALREADY-DECLARED
/// type off the symbol, touching no syntax) and to <see cref="MemberUseScanner"/> (resolves a MEMBER USE — an
/// invocation, a <c>new</c>, a property/field/method-group reference — never a bare type name); the three lenses
/// are independently registered and complementary, so one statement can be caught by more than one of them — no
/// cross-lens deduplication is built here, by design.
/// </summary>
internal static class WrittenNameScanner
{
    /// <summary>
    /// The syntax kinds that hold a name written in source: a bare identifier, a generic name
    /// (<c>List&lt;T&gt;</c>), a dotted qualified name (<c>A.B.C</c> — the TYPE-position spelling, as opposed to a
    /// dotted EXPRESSION's nested member-access chain), and an alias-qualified name (<c>SomeAlias::Name</c>,
    /// including <c>global::Name</c>). There is no fifth "name" syntax kind the C# grammar defines.
    /// </summary>
    private static readonly ImmutableArray<SyntaxKind> NameKinds = ImmutableArray.Create(
        SyntaxKind.IdentifierName,
        SyntaxKind.GenericName,
        SyntaxKind.QualifiedName,
        SyntaxKind.AliasQualifiedName);

    /// <summary>
    /// Registers a syntax node action that resolves every written name to the symbol it binds to, then invokes
    /// <paramref name="inspect"/> so a rule can decide whether to report it. <paramref name="context"/> already
    /// carries the node (<see cref="SyntaxNodeAnalysisContext.Node"/>) and its enclosing symbol
    /// (<see cref="SyntaxNodeAnalysisContext.ContainingSymbol"/>, the same shape <see cref="CompositionPoint"/>'s
    /// caller-site tests already take), so <paramref name="inspect"/> receives only the one thing resolving a name
    /// adds: the symbol it resolved to. The symbol is handed back un-narrowed — a type, a method, a property, a
    /// field, whatever the name bound to — because which kind matters is the caller's own policy: a rule that
    /// cares only about types tests <c>symbol is INamedTypeSymbol</c> itself.
    /// </summary>
    internal static void Register(
        CompilationStartAnalysisContext context,
        Action<SyntaxNodeAnalysisContext, ISymbol> inspect)
    {
        context.RegisterSyntaxNodeAction(syntaxContext => Dispatch(syntaxContext, inspect), NameKinds);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Registers the written-name scan for <paramref name="inspect"/> only when the compilation being analyzed is
    /// the assembly named <paramref name="assemblyName"/>; a compilation that is any other assembly registers
    /// nothing. Mirrors <see cref="MemberUseScanner.RegisterForAssembly"/>'s gate-then-scan calling convention,
    /// built on the existing <see cref="OwnerClass.InAssembly"/> compilation-name gate rather than a second copy of
    /// that check.
    /// </summary>
    internal static void RegisterForAssembly(
        CompilationStartAnalysisContext context,
        string assemblyName,
        Action<SyntaxNodeAnalysisContext, ISymbol> inspect)
    {
        if (!OwnerClass.InAssembly(assemblyName)(context.Compilation))
        {
            return;
        }

        Register(context, inspect);
    }

    private static void Dispatch(SyntaxNodeAnalysisContext context, Action<SyntaxNodeAnalysisContext, ISymbol> inspect)
    {
        // A dotted or alias-qualified name is ONE logical reference spread over nested nodes that all resolve to
        // the SAME symbol: in `A.B.C`, the outer QualifiedName and its trailing "C" identifier both resolve to C.
        // Only the outermost node of such a chain is dispatched, so one written reference is inspected once, not
        // once per dotted segment. A name embedded elsewhere inside it — a generic type argument, an array element —
        // is a SEPARATE reference with its own resolution and is dispatched independently.
        if (IsPartOfEnclosingName(context.Node))
        {
            return;
        }

        ISymbol? symbol = context.SemanticModel.GetSymbolInfo(context.Node, context.CancellationToken).Symbol;
        if (symbol is not null)
        {
            inspect(context, symbol);
        }
    }

    private static bool IsPartOfEnclosingName(SyntaxNode node)
    {
        return node.Parent switch
        {
            QualifiedNameSyntax qualified => node == qualified.Left || node == qualified.Right,
            AliasQualifiedNameSyntax aliased => node == aliased.Name,
            _ => false,
        };
    }
}
```

Every API used is verified live, not assumed: `context.SemanticModel.GetSymbolInfo(SyntaxNode, CancellationToken)` resolves to `Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.ModelExtensions.GetSymbolInfo` (confirmed by reflecting the restored 4.14.0 package — this is the language-agnostic overload taking a plain `SyntaxNode`, distinct from the C#-specific `CSharpExtensions.GetSymbolInfo(ExpressionSyntax, ...)` overloads that would need a cast); `RegisterSyntaxNodeAction` has an `ImmutableArray<TLanguageKindEnum>` overload (confirmed by reflection), matching `MemberUseScanner.MemberUseKinds`'s existing pattern so one registration serves all four kinds; `SyntaxNodeAnalysisContext.ContainingSymbol` exists and is typed `ISymbol`, identically to `OperationAnalysisContext.ContainingSymbol` (confirmed by reflection) — meaning the ALREADY-APPROVED method-level `CompositionPoint`/`OwnerClass.EnclosedBy` caller-site check plugs into this scanner's context with zero adaptation.

Deliberately NOT added: a `Describe(ISymbol)` formatter (unlike `MemberUseScanner.Describe`) — that helper exists only because `MemberUseScanner` splits one member-use into two values (member + declaring type) needing combination; this scanner hands back one resolved symbol, and `symbol.ToDisplayString()` is the whole of what a caller needs, so a wrapper would be an unused, speculative addition.

ILLUSTRATIVE WIRING (RULE-PHASE's job, not built here, shown only to prove the surface is directly usable): AG0041 calls `WrittenNameScanner.RegisterForAssembly(context, CliAssembly.CompiledName, Inspect)` and again with `TestAssembly.TestHelpersName`, mirroring how AG0023/AG0029 already call `MemberUseScanner.RegisterForAssembly` twice each for their two gates. AG0023's and AG0029's Engine gates each add one `WrittenNameScanner.RegisterForAssembly(context, EngineAssembly.Name, Inspect)` call beside their existing `MemberUseScanner` call, narrowing to `symbol is INamedTypeSymbol` and reusing the same door-type-plus-caller-site exception their member-access `Inspect` already applies. None of the three needs a fourth registration or a new syntax-kind case to reach any position Acceptance 9/10/20/21 name — the eleven positions are already reached by the one registration above.

PROPOSAL: `analyzers/AgentGuard.Analyzers/WrittenNameScanner.cs`, class `WrittenNameScanner`, owning the written-name half exactly as contract.md assigns it (DeclaredTypeScanner keeps the carried-type half — not reopened here).

THE CORE DESIGN DECISION. The contract's named failure is "narrowing the lens to the positions an existing scanner already handles." I read the one existing scanner that shape-matches this job — AG0007 (`ContractConcreteTypeMustNotBeCastToAnalyzer.cs:49-53`) — and confirmed it IS that risk made concrete: it registers four hand-picked `SyntaxKind`s (CastExpression, AsExpression, DeclarationPattern, TypePattern) and extracts a `TypeSyntax` per kind. That covers 4 of the 11+ positions the contract lists for AG0041/AG0023/AG0029 and structurally cannot cover the rest (typeof, nameof, generic argument, generic constraint, base list, attribute argument, using alias, array/pointer element, every declaration position) without someone remembering to add each one by hand later — mirroring AG0007's shape would BE the corner-cut.

The fix is to register by syntax NODE KIND, not by hand-enumerated POSITION: `SyntaxKind.IdentifierName` and `SyntaxKind.GenericName` — the two concrete kinds of the abstract `SimpleNameSyntax`. Every position the contract lists bottoms out, at its leaf, at one of exactly these two kinds; there is no eleventh position to forget because the list was never an enumeration. This is also why it must be syntax-based (`RegisterSyntaxNodeAction`), not operation-based like `MemberUseScanner`: a generic constraint (`where T : Foo`), a base-list entry, and an attribute-argument type have no `IOperation` representation at all — they're declaration-time, not executable — so an operation-based scan is structurally incapable of seeing them regardless of how many operation kinds it registers.

No separate registration on `QualifiedNameSyntax` or `MemberAccessExpressionSyntax` is needed or added. I verified this against the actual production code it must cover: `src/AgentGuard.Boundaries/SystemServices.cs:90-91` —
```
CrossPlatformAdapters adapters = CrossPlatformAdapters.Create();
IPlatformServices platform = global::AgentGuard.CrossPlatform.PlatformServices.Create();
```
`global::AgentGuard.CrossPlatform.PlatformServices.Create()` parses as nested `MemberAccessExpressionSyntax`, but "PlatformServices" is ITSELF a leaf `IdentifierNameSyntax` node in that tree (the `.Expression` of the innermost access), visited and resolved independently of its wrapping shape. Registering on the wrapper too would report the same resolved symbol a second time from the same statement — a duplicate-report bug, not extra coverage. This also means "through a using alias" and even an un-listed "through a using-static directive" are caught for free: the referenced name's leaf is still an IdentifierName/GenericName node wherever it sits in a `using` directive's `NameSyntax`.

THE PUBLIC SURFACE (mirrors `MemberUseScanner.Register`/`RegisterForAssembly` deliberately — same gate-then-dispatch shape, unfiltered callback, caller owns all policy and reporting, nothing baked in):

```csharp
// Copyright (c) 4thWAIV. All rights reserved.

using System;
using System.Collections.Immutable;
using Microsoft.CodeAnalysis;
using Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp;
using Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp.Syntax;
using Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.Diagnostics;

namespace AgentGuard.Analyzers;

/// <summary>
/// Resolves every name the source WRITES — an identifier or a generic name, wherever the C# grammar allows one —
/// to the symbol it binds to, and hands each resolved (name, symbol) pair to a rule's inspector. This is the
/// "written name" lens: it catches a type or a member wherever its name is written — typeof, nameof, a cast, an
/// 'is'/'case' pattern, a generic type argument, a generic constraint, a base or interface list, an attribute
/// argument, a using alias, an array or pointer element, and every declaration position — because every one of
/// those positions is represented, at the leaf, as an <see cref="IdentifierNameSyntax"/> or
/// <see cref="GenericNameSyntax"/> node (the two concrete kinds of <see cref="SimpleNameSyntax"/>); no other
/// syntax kind carries a written name. Registering by NODE KIND rather than a hand-picked list of syntactic
/// positions — the shape <see cref="ContractConcreteTypeMustNotBeCastToAnalyzer"/> uses for its own narrower,
/// four-position job — is what makes coverage exhaustive by construction: any form that writes a name still
/// bottoms out at one of these two kinds, so no position is missed by omission the way a per-position list can be.
///
/// Sibling of <see cref="DeclaredTypeScanner"/>, not a competitor: that walks the type a symbol or operation
/// CARRIES without being named (a field's, property's, or local's declared type, including an inferred
/// <c>var</c>); this walks every name the source actually WRITES. Neither subsumes the other. AG0041, AG0023's
/// Engine gate, and AG0029's Engine gate each compose both; this is the one place either rule reaches for the
/// written-name half, so it is never re-implemented inline a second time.
///
/// No registration is made on <see cref="QualifiedNameSyntax"/> or <see cref="MemberAccessExpressionSyntax"/>:
/// each exposes its own right-hand/member name as a nested <see cref="SimpleNameSyntax"/> node that this scanner
/// already visits and resolves independently — registering the wrapper too would report the same resolved symbol
/// a second time from the same written occurrence.
/// </summary>
internal static class WrittenNameScanner
{
    // The two concrete SimpleNameSyntax kinds. See the class remarks for why this is exhaustive by construction.
    private static readonly ImmutableArray<SyntaxKind> NameSyntaxKinds =
        ImmutableArray.Create(SyntaxKind.IdentifierName, SyntaxKind.GenericName);

    /// <summary>
    /// Registers a syntax node action that resolves every identifier and generic name in the compilation to the
    /// symbol it binds to, then invokes <paramref name="inspect"/> for each name that resolves. A name the
    /// semantic model cannot bind to exactly one symbol resolves to no symbol and is skipped.
    /// </summary>
    internal static void Register(
        CompilationStartAnalysisContext context,
        Action<SyntaxNodeAnalysisContext, SimpleNameSyntax, ISymbol> inspect)
    {
        context.RegisterSyntaxNodeAction(nodeContext => Dispatch(nodeContext, inspect), NameSyntaxKinds);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Registers the written-name scan for <paramref name="inspect"/> only when the compilation being analyzed is
    /// the assembly named <paramref name="assemblyName"/>. Mirrors
    /// <see cref="MemberUseScanner.RegisterForAssembly"/>'s gate-then-scan shape so a rule composing both lenses
    /// writes structurally identical setup for each.
    /// </summary>
    internal static void RegisterForAssembly(
        CompilationStartAnalysisContext context,
        string assemblyName,
        Action<SyntaxNodeAnalysisContext, SimpleNameSyntax, ISymbol> inspect)
    {
        if (!string.Equals(context.Compilation.AssemblyName, assemblyName, StringComparison.Ordinal))
        {
            return;
        }

        Register(context, inspect);
    }

    private static void Dispatch(
        SyntaxNodeAnalysisContext context,
        Action<SyntaxNodeAnalysisContext, SimpleNameSyntax, ISymbol> inspect)
    {
        var name = (SimpleNameSyntax)context.Node;
        ISymbol? symbol = context.SemanticModel.GetSymbolInfo(name, context.CancellationToken).Symbol;
        if (symbol is not null)
        {
            inspect(context, name, symbol);
        }
    }
}
```

