RULING — does AG0029's carried-type door exemption need a production case? No, and it must never be given one. Verified directly against the live `Create()` body (`src/AgentGuard.Boundaries/SystemServices.cs:86-107`): it declares no field, property, parameter, or local of type `PlatformServices` anywhere — the local built from the per-OS factory is declared `IPlatformServices` (line 91), the Abstractions interface, never the concrete per-OS type. `PlatformServices` appears in `Create()` exactly once, as the written qualified-call receiver — a written-name-lens catch, not a carried-type-lens one. `CrossPlatformAdapters` genuinely is a carried type today (line 90), so AG0023's carried-type accept path has a real statement to point a test at; AG0029's structurally does not, and the contract's own freeze on `Create()`'s shape means it never will under this contract. That does NOT mean the exemption is optional to build: Acceptance 21 requires the AG0029 tests to "prove the same [as AG0023] for PlatformServices... including as the declared type of a local," so the wiring must exist and be proven — entirely by a synthetic fixture (a fabricated method body declaring a `PlatformServices`-typed local inside a simulated `Create()`), exactly like most of the positions Acceptance 9/20/21 already require and that have no production occurrence either (this is normal for a defensive rule proving what it WOULD catch, not a gap — every existing AG0023/29 test already builds a fabricated subject compilation, never points at real `src/`). This ruling forecloses two specific corner-cuts: (1) silently dropping AG0029's carried-type accept-test because "it never happens for real," leaving AG0023 and AG0029 asymmetric — exactly the "three consuming rules drift their own coverage" failure named in the brief; and (2) editing `SystemServices.Create()`'s production body to manufacture a real occurrence to test against — a direct breach of the contract's shape-freeze and no-behavior-change con